                <pb facs="0109" n="29" sameAs="frus:frus1902/pg29"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr. McCormick.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1902-04-07"><hi rendition="#italic">April 7,
                        1902</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 40.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your telegram of the 6th
                    ultimo, and to your confirmatory dispatch of the following day’s date, reporting
                    the intention of the Austro-Hungarian Government to raise its mission at
                    Washington to the rank of an embassy, you are instructed to express to the
                    minister of foreign affairs the gratification with which the President has heard
                    of this new proof of the Emperor’s friendly disposition toward the United
                    States, and to say that in due time it will give him pleasure to reciprocate by
                    appointing, in token of his high regard and esteem for the person of the Emperor
                    and of his good wishes for the people of Austro-Hungary, as ambassador to Vienna
                    a gentleman who he is sure will be agreeable to His Majesty.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
